 "prompt_text": "[gMASK]<sop><|user|>Write a Python function that merges overlapping intervals. Explain its algorithm, edge cases, and time complexity. Include examples.<|assistant|><think></think>",
 "completion_text": "# Merging Overlapping Intervals\n\n## The Problem\n\nGiven a collection of intervals like `[(1, 4), (2, 6), (8, 10)]`, merge all overlapping intervals so the result is `[(1, 6), (8, 10)]`.\n\n## The Solution\n\n```python\ndef merge_intervals(intervals):\n    \"\"\"\n    Merge overlapping intervals.\n\n    Args:\n        intervals: A list of (start, end) tuples or lists.\n\n    Returns:\n        A list of merged (start, end) tuples, sorted by start.\n\n    Raises:\n        ValueError: If any interval has start > end.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not intervals:\n        return []\n\n    # Validate input\n    for start, end in intervals:\n        if start > end:\n            raise ValueError(f\"Invalid interval: start {start} > end {end}\")\n\n    # Step 1: Sort by start value\n    sorted_intervals = sorted(intervals, key=lambda x: x[0])\n\n    merged = [list(sorted_intervals[0])]\n\n    # Step 2: Sweep through and merge as we go\n    for start, end in sorted_intervals[1:]:\n        last = merged[-1]\n\n        if start <= last[1]:          # Overlaps (or touches) the last merged interval\n            last[1] = max(last[1], end)  # Extend it if needed\n        else:                          # No overlap \u2014 start a new interval\n            merged.append([start, end])\n\n    return [tuple(interval) for interval in merged]\n```\n\n## The Algorithm Explained\n\nThe key insight: **if intervals are sorted by start value, any interval can only overlap with the most recently merged interval.** This turns a potentially O(n\u00b2) pairwise-comparison problem into a single linear sweep.\n\n1. **Sort** the intervals by their start value \u2014",
